1
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:06,790
Right now, a machine built in 1977 is still

2
00:00:06,790 --> 00:00:09,920
sending us messages from more than twenty-four

3
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:11,930
billion kilometres away.

4
00:00:13,268 --> 00:00:16,258
And on Mars, another machine has said nothing

5
00:00:16,258 --> 00:00:17,968
at all for eight years.

6
00:00:19,549 --> 00:00:21,879
Same agency. Same promise.

7
00:00:21,879 --> 00:00:23,499
Two very different endings.

8
00:00:25,084 --> 00:00:27,084
We're a team from Bangladesh.

9
00:00:28,491 --> 00:00:30,481
We're a developer, a researcher,

10
00:00:30,481 --> 00:00:32,531
a designer and a storyteller.

11
00:00:34,111 --> 00:00:36,221
We kept asking one question:

12
00:00:36,221 --> 00:00:37,741
when a mission goes quiet,

13
00:00:37,741 --> 00:00:39,331
where does its story go?

14
00:00:40,990 --> 00:00:43,720
Our answer is a website called Abandoned,

15
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:45,600
But Not Forgotten.

16
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:49,480
Since the 1960s, NASA has left hardware on

17
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:52,570
the Moon, on Mars, and in deep space.

18
00:00:53,447 --> 00:00:56,087
The facts exist. But they're scattered across

19
00:00:56,087 --> 00:00:58,197
mission pages, press releases,

20
00:00:58,197 --> 00:00:59,367
and PDFs.

21
00:01:00,249 --> 00:01:02,209
And one word hides the truth:

22
00:01:02,209 --> 00:01:03,219
abandoned.

23
00:01:04,128 --> 00:01:06,728
Nobody drove these machines home.

24
00:01:06,728 --> 00:01:09,570
They worked until something stopped them.

25
00:01:09,570 --> 00:01:10,540
Dust.

26
00:01:11,194 --> 00:01:12,214
Cold.

27
00:01:12,860 --> 00:01:14,280
Fading power.

28
00:01:14,974 --> 00:01:16,004
Distance.

29
00:01:17,018 --> 00:01:19,728
Opportunity was built to last ninety Martian

30
00:01:19,728 --> 00:01:23,098
days. It worked for more than fourteen years.

31
00:01:23,905 --> 00:01:27,795
Then, in 2018, a global dust storm blocked

32
00:01:27,795 --> 00:01:29,326
its sunlight.

33
00:01:29,326 --> 00:01:31,936
Engineers sent more than a thousand recovery

34
00:01:31,936 --> 00:01:34,366
commands. Nothing came back.

35
00:01:35,168 --> 00:01:37,188
That's the part that matters most:

36
00:01:37,188 --> 00:01:38,738
why a mission ended.

37
00:01:38,738 --> 00:01:41,088
And it's the part hardest to find.

38
00:01:42,121 --> 00:01:45,111
A student shouldn't need ten tabs to understand it.

39
00:01:45,748 --> 00:01:47,778
So we built one place for it.

40
00:01:47,778 --> 00:01:51,398
Fifteen missions, from 1966 to today,

41
00:01:51,398 --> 00:01:55,038
across the Moon, Mars and deep space.

42
00:01:55,038 --> 00:01:56,748
Scroll the homepage,

43
00:01:56,748 --> 00:01:59,498
and the story unfolds scene by scene,

44
00:01:59,498 --> 00:02:02,472
from leaving Earth to the final silence.

45
00:02:02,472 --> 00:02:05,142
Follow six decades on the timeline,

46
00:02:05,142 --> 00:02:08,263
or find every machine on the hardware map.

47
00:02:08,263 --> 00:02:11,063
Our Final Signal page separates four moments

48
00:02:11,063 --> 00:02:13,553
most people mix up: launch,

49
00:02:13,553 --> 00:02:15,613
arrival, last contact,

50
00:02:15,613 --> 00:02:17,132
and official closure.

51
00:02:17,132 --> 00:02:20,875
Because lost and finished are not the same thing.

52
00:02:20,875 --> 00:02:24,490
Pick a spacecraft and turn it around in 3D.

53
00:02:24,490 --> 00:02:26,500
Or play Mission Detective.

54
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:28,200
Read the clues about Spirit,

55
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:30,490
Opportunity, or Surveyor 3,

56
00:02:30,490 --> 00:02:32,500
and work out what happened.

57
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:34,660
There's a quiz, a mission comparison,

58
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:36,819
and a 3D game too.

59
00:02:36,819 --> 00:02:40,782
Every fact comes from NASA and JPL sources.

60
00:02:40,782 --> 00:02:43,682
And when a machine speaks in first person,

61
00:02:43,682 --> 00:02:46,992
we label it clearly as creative interpretation,

62
00:02:46,992 --> 00:02:49,531
never as a real transmission.

63
00:02:49,531 --> 00:02:50,991
Why does this matter?

64
00:02:52,234 --> 00:02:54,924
For a student, it turns space history from

65
00:02:54,924 --> 00:02:57,164
a list of dates into a set of stories with

66
00:02:57,164 --> 00:02:59,314
an ending, and a reason.

67
00:03:00,463 --> 00:03:02,843
It's built with plain HTML,

68
00:03:02,843 --> 00:03:05,503
CSS, and JavaScript.

69
00:03:05,503 --> 00:03:07,323
No framework, no backend,

70
00:03:07,323 --> 00:03:09,233
and most of it works offline.

71
00:03:10,085 --> 00:03:12,255
So a classroom with a weak connection can

72
00:03:12,255 --> 00:03:15,405
still open it. That matters where we live.

73
00:03:16,625 --> 00:03:19,705
And it's honest. Sources are on the site,

74
00:03:19,705 --> 00:03:22,195
and the line between fact and imagination

75
00:03:22,195 --> 00:03:23,605
is never blurred.

76
00:03:24,709 --> 00:03:25,939
This is a first version,

77
00:03:25,939 --> 00:03:27,669
with fifteen missions.

78
00:03:27,669 --> 00:03:30,199
To take it further, we need three things:

79
00:03:31,115 --> 00:03:33,825
experts to review and expand the archive,

80
00:03:34,859 --> 00:03:38,739
access to more NASA imagery and 3D surface data,

81
00:03:39,707 --> 00:03:40,897
and a Bangla version,

82
00:03:40,897 --> 00:03:42,567
with teacher resources,

83
00:03:42,567 --> 00:03:44,447
so schools here can use it.

84
00:03:46,947 --> 00:03:48,937
Voyager is still talking.

85
00:03:48,937 --> 00:03:50,707
Opportunity went quiet.

86
00:03:51,555 --> 00:03:54,255
Our job is to make sure both are heard.

87
00:03:55,366 --> 00:03:57,296
Abandoned, But Not Forgotten.
